  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1131 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nSimilar weather compared to the past few days will continue as an\narid continental air mass remains parked over the eastern CONUS.\nWednesday morning's lows are expected to be slightly warmer than\nthe past few nights, mainly owing to the presence of scattered\nmid-level cloud cover associated with a plume of moderate mid-\nlevel moisture advecting in from the plains. So, expect lows in\nthe middle to low 40s tonight for most of the region and some\npotential for patchy fog, mainly near the Tennessee River.\n\nSurface humidity will quickly plummet after sunrise. All three\nnearby upper air sites (LZK, OHX, JAN) display very dry dewpoints\n(< 0 C) just above 850 mb, which will promptly mix down dewpoints\nthrough the day. Highs are forecast to reach back into the 70s\nand low 80s, leading to afternoon and evening relative humidity\nvalues between 20 and 30 percent. Fire danger potential will\nremain elevated as a consequence, especially with very dry fuels\npresent throughout the area.\n\nThe upper pattern will be evolving through the first half of the\nforecast with a ridge slowly amplifying over the Plains through\nthe end of the week with an omega block pattern developing.\nSurface high pressure will remain locked in place through this\ntime period, keeping a persistent forecast in place. Therefore,\ndaily highs are expected to stick to the 70s with lows in the 50s\nthrough at least Friday. Ensembles are confident that the ridge\naxis will shift east and into the region this weekend, centered\nover the Mississippi River Valley. However, temperatures are not\nlikely to budge much from this week with the interquartile range\nof NBM high temperatures Saturday and Sunday only around 3 F.\nAdditionally, the variation of highs from day to day is also only\n+/- 1 F, likely a manifestation of the lengthening nights this\ntime of the year. Regardless of the temperature forecast,\nprecipitation is not expected due to the ridge and the recycling\nof dry, continental air near the surface, similar to this week.\nForecast confidence degrades rapidly by Monday next week as the\nomega block fully breaks down. So, it is hard to say at this time\nif we will be able to break out of the dry weather or the Mid-\nSouth will remain parched evermore.",
